    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_0199dAifCydwAdfMmbq2u91L",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N1",
                "spans": [
                  "P3",
                  "P17",
                  "P18",
                  "P19",
                  "P20",
                  "P142",
                  "P143"
                ],
                "statement": "Within five days the UK's line moved from welcoming Trump's reciprocal energy ceasefire, which Ukraine would implement once a start date was confirmed, to declaring that Russia had no interest in peace, so London now uses the US ceasefire call as a test it says Russia is failing and as grounds for pressing partners to raise sanctions on Russia.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N2",
                "spans": [
                  "P1",
                  "P2",
                  "P10",
                  "P24"
                ],
                "statement": "So far the promise to go 'further and faster' rests on modest near-term money: £10 million of a £45 million missile package brought forward inside an existing £1.6 billion four-year commitment, while further sanctions, military and fiscal packages have only been commissioned.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N3",
                "spans": [
                  "P58",
                  "P61",
                  "P62",
                  "P111",
                  "P113",
                  "P115",
                  "P116",
                  "P128",
                  "P129",
                  "P132"
                ],
                "statement": "Burnham's Scottish offer exports the model he ran as a mayor and is running in England (public control and local procurement, mayoral innovation funding, localised employment support), and because it is framed as moving power out of Holyrood as well as Westminster, it is also a challenge to the Scottish Government's centralism; the first test is the National Economic Council meeting he promised within a couple of weeks.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "assessments": [],
                "ref": "N4",
                "spans": [
                  "P46",
                  "P50",
                  "P55",
                  "P56",
                  "P109",
                  "P110"
                ],
                "statement": "The 20 November UK-EU summit links the government's foreign and domestic messages, since the food and drink deal is sold both in the Commons as UK-wide growth and in Scotland as a boost to Scottish farming, and Burnham has already said he wants a longer-term dialogue on the EU relationship after it.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "Andy Burnham has been Prime Minister since 20 July 2026, a month after he stepped down as Mayor of Greater Manchester. That background helps explain this week's material."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "P3",
                  "P17",
                  "P18",
                  "P19",
                  "P20"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "On Ukraine, the order of events matters. Responding to the US-Russia agreement of 9 October, a government spokesperson said Russia had repeatedly rejected ceasefires. On 12 October Burnham welcomed President Trump's reciprocal energy ceasefire and said Zelenskyy had promised to implement it once a start date was confirmed. By 14 October the two leaders were pointing to Russian attacks since Trump's call and saying Russia had no interest in peace."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N1",
                  "P143",
                  "P142"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "The UK is now treating the US ceasefire call as a test it says Russia is failing. It is using that to press partners to step up sanctions and make Putin respect it."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N2",
                  "P10",
                  "P24",
                  "P1"
                ],
                "stories": [
                  {
                    "span": "P1",
                    "story": [
                      "P138",
                      "P21"
                    ]
                  }
                ],
                "text": "The scale needs watching. Burnham promised to go 'further and faster', but the money brought forward so far is £10 million of a £45 million missile package, inside a £1.6 billion commitment spread over four years. The larger sanctions, military and fiscal packages have only been asked for."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N3",
                  "P62",
                  "P61",
                  "P111",
                  "P128"
                ],
                "stories": [
                  {
                    "span": "P62",
                    "story": [
                      "P136",
                      "P137"
                    ]
                  }
                ],
                "text": "In Scotland, Burnham offered the Scottish Government what he built as mayor and is now doing in England: buses under public control bought from Falkirk, localised employment support and local say over innovation money. But he put it as pushing power out of Holyrood as well as Westminster, and called Scotland one of Europe's most centralised nations. That makes the offer a challenge to the Scottish Government as well as an invitation."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N3",
                  "P129",
                  "P147"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "The first test is the National Economic Council meeting with the devolved First Ministers, which Burnham promised within a couple of weeks. He says he has worked with John Swinney for a long time."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "N4",
                  "P46",
                  "P109",
                  "P55"
                ],
                "stories": [],
                "text": "Both agendas lead to the UK-EU summit in Brussels on 20 November. The food and drink deal is sold in the Commons as UK-wide growth and in Scotland as help for Scottish farming. Burnham has already said he wants a longer-term discussion of the EU relationship after the summit."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
