                  "stories": [],
                  "text": "The UK is now treating the US ceasefire call as a test it says Russia is failing. It is using that to press partners to step up sanctions and make Putin respect it."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "N2",
                    "P10",
                    "P24",
                    "P1"
                  ],
                  "stories": [
                    {
                      "span": "P1",
                      "story": [
                        "P138",
                        "P21"
                      ]
                    }
                  ],
                  "text": "The scale needs watching. Burnham promised to go 'further and faster', but the money brought forward so far is £10 million of a £45 million missile package, inside a £1.6 billion commitment spread over four years. The larger sanctions, military and fiscal packages have only been asked for."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "N3",
                    "P62",
                    "P61",
                    "P111",
                    "P128"
                  ],
                  "stories": [
                    {
                      "span": "P62",
                      "story": [
                        "P136",
                        "P137"
                      ]
                    }
                  ],
                  "text": "In Scotland, Burnham offered the Scottish Government what he built as mayor and is now doing in England: buses under public control bought from Falkirk, localised employment support and local say over innovation money. But he put it as pushing power out of Holyrood as well as Westminster, and called Scotland one of Europe's most centralised nations. That makes the offer a challenge to the Scottish Government as well as an invitation."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "N3",
                    "P129",
                    "P147"
                  ],
                  "stories": [],
                  "text": "The first test is the National Economic Council meeting with the devolved First Ministers, which Burnham promised within a couple of weeks. He says he has worked with John Swinney for a long time."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "N4",
                    "P46",
                    "P109",
                    "P55"
                  ],
                  "stories": [],
                  "text": "Both agendas lead to the UK-EU summit in Brussels on 20 November. The food and drink deal is sold in the Commons as UK-wide growth and in Scotland as help for Scottish farming. Burnham has already said he wants a longer-term discussion of the EU relationship after the summit."
                }
              ]
            },
